.page glob 7 file name patterns

# DESCRIPTION
A glob is a pattern for file names, not a regular expression. It is used by gapsh(1) when it expands words such as `*.c`, in the patterns of `case` and of `${NAME#pattern}`, by `find -name`, and by `grep --include`.

# PATTERNS
@ *
    Any string of characters, including none.
@ ?
    Any single character.
@ [abc], [a-z]
    Any one of the characters or ranges listed.
@ [!abc], [^abc]
    Any one character not listed.
@ \\c
    The character c, taken literally.

In a file name expansion a slash must be matched by a slash and a leading dot by a dot: `*` does not match `.profile`. If nothing matches, the word is left as it was typed. Quoting a character takes away its special meaning.

The matching of `find -name` is on the base name; `find -path` matches the whole path. `grep --include` understands only `*` and `?`.

# EXAMPLES
    ls /bin/p*
    ls *.[ch]
    case "$f" in *.txt|*.md) echo text;; esac
    f=archive.tar.gz; echo ${f%%.*}

# SEE ALSO
gapsh-language(7), find(1), regex(7)
